cs-04.theory:
# Making the program decide

In the previous lesson the program always did the same calculations. Now it will choose **different paths** depending on the data.

## Comparisons and bool

Comparisons produce a `bool` (`true` or `false`):

```csharp
nota >= 7     // greater than or equal
nota < 5      // less than
x == 0        // equal (two = signs)
x != 0        // different
```

Careful: `=` **assigns** a value; `==` **compares**. In C#, `if (x = 0)` doesn't even compile, which avoids a classic error from other languages.

## if, else if, else

```csharp
if (temperatura > 30)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Calor");
}
else if (temperatura > 15)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Agradável");
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Frio");
}
```

The conditions are tested **in order**, from top to bottom. The first true one runs its block and the others are **ignored**. That is why the order matters: in `else if (temperatura > 15)` we already know the temperature is **not** greater than 30, so we don't need to repeat that.

The braces `{ }` delimit the block. With a single statement they are optional, but always using them avoids bugs when you later add a second line.

## Combining conditions

- `&&` (AND): true only if **both** are true.
- `||` (OR): true if **at least one** is true.
- `!` (NOT): inverts.

```csharp
if (idade >= 18 && temCarteira)
    Console.WriteLine("Pode dirigir");

if (nota < 0 || nota > 10)
    Console.WriteLine("Nota inválida");
```

C# uses **short-circuit evaluation**: in `a && b`, if `a` is false, `b` isn't even evaluated. This lets you safely write `if (b != 0 && a / b > 2)`.

## Validate first

A good habit is to handle the invalid cases **first**. That way the rest of the code can assume the data is correct.

## Common mistakes

- Writing `if (nota >= 7);` with a semicolon: the `;` ends the `if` and the block always runs.
- Wrong order: testing `nota >= 5` before `nota >= 7` makes a grade of 9 fall into "Recuperação".
- Comparing `double` with `==` after calculations: values like `0.1 + 0.2` are not exactly `0.3`. Prefer `>=` and `<`.

cs-04.task:
Read a grade (a decimal number, with a dot, e.g. `6.5`) and show **one** line:

- `Nota inválida` if the grade is less than 0 or greater than 10;
- `Aprovado` if the grade is greater than or equal to 7;
- `Recuperação` if it is greater than or equal to 5 (and less than 7);
- `Reprovado` otherwise.

Example: input `6.5` produces `Recuperação`.

cs-05.theory:
# Repeating statements

With `if` the program chooses paths. With **loops** it repeats a block many times, without copying code.

## while

`while` repeats **as long as** the condition is true:

```csharp
int contador = 3;
while (contador > 0)
{
    Console.WriteLine(contador);
    contador--;   // same as contador = contador - 1
}
// 3, 2, 1
```

If nothing inside the loop changes the condition, it never ends (an **infinite loop**). Always ask yourself: what makes this loop stop?

## for

When you know **how many times** to repeat, `for` puts the three parts in one place:

```csharp
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
{
    Console.WriteLine(i);   // 0 1 2 3 4
}
```

1. `int i = 0`: runs once, at the start.
2. `i < 5`: tested before each iteration; if false, the loop ends.
3. `i++`: runs at the end of each iteration.

Counting from `0` to `n - 1` is the standard in C#, because arrays (lesson 7) start at index 0.

## Accumulators

The most common pattern with loops is to **accumulate** a result:

```csharp
int n = int.Parse(Console.ReadLine());
int soma = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
{
    int x = int.Parse(Console.ReadLine());
    soma += x;   // soma = soma + x
}
```

Notice: the variable `soma` is declared **outside** the loop. If it were inside, it would be recreated (reset to zero) on every iteration and wouldn't be visible after the loop. This is the concept of **scope**: a variable only exists inside the braces where it was declared.

## Largest and smallest

To find the largest value, start with the **first** element (not with 0, which would fail if all were negative). Another safe option is to start with `int.MinValue`, the smallest possible `int`:

```csharp
int maior = int.MinValue;
// inside the loop:
if (x > maior) maior = x;
```

## break and continue

- `break` leaves the loop immediately.
- `continue` skips to the next iteration.

## Common mistakes

- `i <= n` instead of `i < n`: one iteration too many.
- Initializing the largest with `0`: wrong for lists of only negatives.
- Forgetting the case of 0 elements: the loop doesn't run and "maior" keeps a meaningless value. Handle that case first.

cs-08.theory:
# Modeling data with classes

Until now each piece of data was a loose variable. But in the real world data goes together: a student has a **name** and **grades**. A **class** puts data (state) and behavior (methods) together in a single type, created by you.

## Declaring a class

```csharp
class Aluno
{
    public string Nome { get; }          // read-only property
    private List<int> notas = new List<int>(); // private field

    public Aluno(string nome)            // constructor
    {
        Nome = nome;
    }

    public void AdicionarNota(int nota)
    {
        notas.Add(nota);
    }

    public double Media()
    {
        int soma = 0;
        foreach (int n in notas) soma += n;
        return (double)soma / notas.Count;
    }
}
```

- A **field** (`notas`) stores data inside the object.
- A **property** (`Nome { get; }`) looks like a field from the outside, but controls access: here it can only be read, and it is set in the constructor.
- The **constructor** has the name of the class and runs when the object is created with `new`.
- `public` can be used from outside; `private` only inside the class. Hiding the details (**encapsulation**) lets you change the internal workings without breaking whoever uses the class.

```csharp
Aluno a = new Aluno("Ana");
a.AdicionarNota(8);
a.AdicionarNota(7);
Console.WriteLine(a.Media()); // 7.5
```

Now the methods are **not** `static`: they work with the data of **one specific object**. Each `new Aluno(...)` has its own grades.

## List<T>

`List<T>` is an array that grows: `Add`, `Count`, index access `lista[i]`. The `T` is the type of the elements (`List<int>`, `List<Aluno>`).

## Dictionary<TKey, TValue>

A dictionary associates **keys** with **values** and finds a key in practically constant time, without going through everything:

```csharp
var porNome = new Dictionary<string, Aluno>();
if (!porNome.TryGetValue("Ana", out Aluno aluno))
{
    aluno = new Aluno("Ana");
    porNome["Ana"] = aluno;
}
aluno.AdicionarNota(9);
```

`TryGetValue` returns `true` and fills `aluno` if the key exists. Accessing `porNome["Bia"]` with a nonexistent key throws `KeyNotFoundException`.

**Careful with the order:** the `Dictionary` documentation **does not guarantee** the order in which keys are traversed. When order matters, also store the objects in a `List` in the order they appeared, and use the dictionary only to **find** them quickly.

## References

Classes are **reference types**: the variable holds an "address" of the object. The same `Aluno` can be in the list and in the dictionary at the same time; adding a grade through one path shows up through the other, because it is the **same** object.

## Common mistakes

- Dividing `int` by `int` in the average (lesson 3): use `(double)soma`.
- Forgetting `new` and using a `null` variable: `NullReferenceException`.
- Relying on the dictionary's order.

cs-10.theory:
# Algorithms and the cost of running them

In the previous lessons we learned to **write** programs. Now we will learn to **compare** programs: two correct algorithms can have wildly different costs when the input grows.

## Big-O notation

Measuring in seconds depends on the machine. Instead, we count **basic operations** as a function of the input size `n`, and look only at the term that **dominates** when `n` is large, ignoring constants. Formally, `f(n) = O(g(n))` if there are constants `c` and `n0` such that `f(n) <= c·g(n)` for all `n >= n0`.

- `O(1)`: constant. Accessing `arr[i]`.
- `O(log n)`: logarithmic. Each step **halves** the problem.
- `O(n)`: linear. Walking through the array once.
- `O(n log n)`: the good sorts (merge sort, and `Array.Sort` in practice).
- `O(n²)`: quadratic. Two nested loops over the data.
- `O(2^n)`: exponential, like the Tower of Hanoi from lesson 9.

For `n = 1 000 000`: `log n` is about 20, `n log n` about 20 million, and `n²` is a trillion. The choice of algorithm matters more than the speed of the computer.

## Insertion sort

The idea is that of someone arranging cards in their hand: we keep the beginning of the array **sorted** and insert each new element in the right place, shifting the larger ones one position to the right.

```csharp
static void InsertionSort(int[] v)
{
    for (int i = 1; i < v.Length; i++)
    {
        int atual = v[i];
        int j = i - 1;
        while (j >= 0 && v[j] > atual)
        {
            v[j + 1] = v[j];
            j--;
        }
        v[j + 1] = atual;
    }
}
```

**Invariant**: at the start of each iteration, `v[0..i-1]` is sorted. It holds at the beginning (a single element is sorted) and each iteration preserves it; at the end, `i == v.Length` and the whole array is sorted. This is how you **prove** an algorithm is correct.

**Cost**: in the worst case (array in descending order) the inner loop runs `1 + 2 + ... + (n-1) = n(n-1)/2` times: **O(n²)**. In the best case (already sorted) it is **O(n)**. It is **stable** (equal elements keep their order) and sorts **in place**, with O(1) extra memory. Note the strict `>`: with `>=` it would no longer be stable.

Arrays in C# are reference types: the method changes the very array it received.

## Binary search

In a **sorted** array, we compare the target with the middle element and discard half at each step:

```csharp
int ini = 0, fim = v.Length - 1, achou = -1;
while (ini <= fim)
{
    int meio = ini + (fim - ini) / 2;
    if (v[meio] < alvo) ini = meio + 1;
    else
    {
        if (v[meio] == alvo) achou = meio;
        fim = meio - 1;   // keep searching to the left
    }
}
```

This variant finds the **first** occurrence of the target, even with duplicates. After `k` steps about `n / 2^k` candidates remain, so it takes at most about `log2(n) + 1` steps: **O(log n)**. A million elements: around 20 steps, versus up to a million for linear search.

Why `ini + (fim - ini) / 2` and not `(ini + fim) / 2`? Because the sum can **overflow** the `int` in huge arrays; a real bug that lived for years in famous libraries.

## Is it worth sorting in order to search?

Sorting costs O(n log n) (with a good algorithm) and each search afterwards costs O(log n). For **one** search, the linear O(n) one is cheaper. For **many** searches, sorting once pays off. Thinking this way is the heart of algorithm analysis.

## Common mistakes

- Binary search on an unsorted array: meaningless results.
- `while (ini < fim)` instead of `<=`: misses the single-element case.
- Forgetting `j >= 0` in insertion sort: `IndexOutOfRangeException`.

cs-12.theory:
# Interfaces and polymorphism

In lesson 8 each class was independent. But often different types know how to do **the same thing** in different ways: a rectangle, a circle and a triangle have an area, but each one computes it with its own formula. We want to treat all of them as "a shape" and let each one answer in its own way.

## Interface: a contract

An **interface** lists what a type **promises** to know how to do, without saying how:

```csharp
interface IForma
{
    string Nome { get; }
    double Area();
    double Perimetro();
}
```

By convention, interface names in C# start with `I`. A class **implements** the interface with `:` and must provide every member:

```csharp
class Circulo : IForma
{
    private readonly double raio;
    public Circulo(double raio) { this.raio = raio; }

    public string Nome => "Círculo";
    public double Area() => Math.PI * raio * raio;
    public double Perimetro() => 2 * Math.PI * raio;
}
```

The `=>` syntax (*expression body*) is a shortcut for a one-line method or property: `double Area() => ...;` is the same as `double Area() { return ...; }`. `readonly` prevents the field from changing after the constructor.

## Polymorphism

Now a variable of type `IForma` can hold **any** shape:

```csharp
var formas = new List<IForma> { new Circulo(1), new Retangulo(3, 4) };
foreach (IForma f in formas)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"{f.Nome}: {f.Area():F2}");
}
```

The loop doesn't know (and doesn't need to know) which shape is which: at run time, each object runs **its own** `Area()`. That is **polymorphism** ("many forms"). To add a hexagon tomorrow, you just create the class; the loop stays the same. That is the **open/closed** principle: open for extension, closed for modification.

## Inheritance and abstract classes

Another way to share behaviour is **inheritance**: `class Quadrado : Retangulo` inherits everything from `Retangulo`. An **abstract class** sits halfway: it can have ready-made code and `abstract` methods that the children must write (with `override`). A class can inherit from **only one** class, but implement **several** interfaces. When in doubt, prefer interfaces: they couple less.

## Heron's formula

The area of a triangle with sides `a`, `b` and `c` can be computed from the sides alone. With the semi-perimeter `s = (a + b + c) / 2`:

```
area = √( s · (s − a) · (s − b) · (s − c) )
```

In C#: `Math.Sqrt(s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c))`.

## Decimal numbers in the input

`double.Parse("2.5")` depends on the computer's language: in Portuguese, the decimal separator is the comma. To always read with a point, use `double.Parse(text, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)` (with `using System.Globalization;`).

## Common mistakes

- Forgetting to implement a member of the interface: the code doesn't compile.
- Writing `new IForma()`: interfaces cannot be instantiated, only the classes that implement them.

cs-13.theory:
# Generics

You have already used `List<int>`, `List<string>` and `Dictionary<string, Aluno>`. The `<T>` of those classes is a **type parameter**: the same `List` works for any type, without losing the compiler's checks. In this lesson you will **write** your own generic classes and methods.

## The problem

Imagine a stack (the LIFO structure: the last one in is the first one out). Without generics, you would need a `PilhaDeInt`, a `PilhaDeString`... all the same, changing only the type. Using `object` for everything works, but loses safety: nothing prevents mixing numbers and texts, and every read requires a conversion that may fail at run time.

## A generic class

```csharp
class Pilha<T>
{
    private readonly List<T> itens = new List<T>();

    public int Count => itens.Count;

    public void Push(T item) => itens.Add(item);

    public T Pop()
    {
        if (itens.Count == 0) throw new InvalidOperationException("pilha vazia");
        T topo = itens[itens.Count - 1];
        itens.RemoveAt(itens.Count - 1);
        return topo;
    }
}
```

`T` is a "type to be defined". When you write `new Pilha<string>()`, the compiler creates a stack where `T` is `string`: `Push(42)` doesn't even compile. The code was written **once** and works for every type. Removing from the **end** of the list costs O(1), which is why the top stays at the end.

## Generic methods

A method can also have its own type parameter:

```csharp
static List<T> Inverter<T>(IEnumerable<T> itens)
{
    var pilha = new Pilha<T>();
    foreach (T x in itens) pilha.Push(x);
    var resultado = new List<T>();
    while (pilha.Count > 0) resultado.Add(pilha.Pop());
    return resultado;
}
```

Usually C# **infers** the `T` by itself: `Inverter(numeros)` with an `int[]` uses `T = int`. `IEnumerable<T>` is the interface of "anything you can go through with `foreach`": arrays, lists, sets...

## Constraints: where

To find the largest element, we need to **compare** two `T` values. But not every type knows how to compare itself! The `where` constraint requires it:

```csharp
static T Maior<T>(IEnumerable<T> itens) where T : IComparable<T>
{
    ...
    if (x.CompareTo(maior) > 0) maior = x;
    ...
}
```

`a.CompareTo(b)` returns a negative number if `a < b`, zero if they are equal and a positive one if `a > b`. `int`, `double`, `string` and `DateTime` implement `IComparable<T>`; a class of yours can implement it too. Other constraints: `where T : class` (reference type), `where T : new()` (has a parameterless constructor).

## Why generics are fast

In C#, `List<int>` stores the `int` values directly, without wrapping them in objects (*boxing*). That is why generics are safe **and** fast at the same time. The whole .NET collections library (`List<T>`, `Dictionary<TKey, TValue>`, `HashSet<T>`, `Queue<T>`, `Stack<T>`) is built this way.

## Common mistakes

- Calling `CompareTo` without the `where T : IComparable<T>` constraint: it doesn't compile.
- Comparing generics with `>`: the operators don't exist for an arbitrary `T`.

cs-15.theory:
# Records

In lesson 8 you saw that classes are **reference types**: two variables can point to the **same** object. That has a surprising consequence:

```csharp
class PontoClasse { public int X; public int Y; }

var a = new PontoClasse { X = 1, Y = 2 };
var b = new PontoClasse { X = 1, Y = 2 };
Console.WriteLine(a == b);   // False! They are two different objects
```

For a class, `==` asks "is it the **same object**?" (reference equality). But for a point, a date or an amount of money, what matters is the **content**: two points (1, 2) are the same point. That is what **records** are for.

## Declaring a record

```csharp
record Ponto(int X, int Y);
```

That single line creates a type with:

- read-only (**immutable**) properties `X` and `Y`;
- a constructor `new Ponto(1, 2)`;
- **value equality**: `new Ponto(1, 2) == new Ponto(1, 2)` is `True`;
- a `GetHashCode` consistent with that equality (that is why it works in a `HashSet` and as a `Dictionary` key);
- a readable `ToString()`: `Ponto { X = 1, Y = 2 }`.

With an ordinary class, you would write dozens of lines to get all that, and it is easy to get it wrong.

## Immutability and with

The properties of a positional record cannot be changed after it is created. To "change" a value, you create a **modified copy** with `with`:

```csharp
var p = new Ponto(0, 0);
var q = p with { Y = p.Y + 1 };   // Ponto { X = 0, Y = 1 }; p is still (0, 0)
```

Immutable objects are easier to reason about: nobody changes your point "behind your back", and they can be shared between several parts of the program (or several threads) safely.

## Records can have methods too

```csharp
record Ponto(int X, int Y)
{
    public int Manhattan() => Math.Abs(X) + Math.Abs(Y);
}
```

The **Manhattan distance** is how many blocks you walk in a grid city: |x| + |y|, with no diagonal shortcuts.

## HashSet: a set without repetition

`HashSet<T>` stores elements **without repetition** and answers "is it already there?" in O(1) on average (it is a hash table). The `Add` method returns `false` when the element was already there, which is perfect for detecting repetitions:

```csharp
var visitados = new HashSet<Ponto> { new Ponto(0, 0) };
bool novo = visitados.Add(new Ponto(0, 0));   // false: it was already there
```

That only works because the record compares by **value**. With an ordinary class, each `new` would be a different element.

## record struct

There is also the `record struct`, which is a **value** type (copied whole on every assignment, like `int`), good for small data. The ordinary `record`, used in this lesson, is still a reference type, but with value equality.

cs-16.theory:
# Delegates, lambdas and events

Until now, variables held **data**. In C#, a variable can also hold **a function**. That lets you pass behaviour as a parameter, build rules at run time and notify other parts of the program when something happens.

## Delegates: the type of a function

A **delegate** is a type that describes the "shape" of a function: which parameters it takes and what it returns. The library already includes the most common ones:

| Type | Means |
|---|---|
| `Func<int, int>` | takes an `int`, returns an `int` |
| `Func<double, double, double>` | takes two `double` values, returns a `double` |
| `Func<int, bool>` | takes an `int`, returns `bool` (a test) |
| `Action<string>` | takes a `string`, returns nothing |
| `Action<int, int>` | takes two `int` values, returns nothing |

In `Func`, the **last** type is always the return.

## Lambdas: functions without a name

```csharp
Func<int, int> dobro = x => x * 2;
Func<int, bool> ehPar = x => x % 2 == 0;
Action<string> saudar = nome => Console.WriteLine($"Oi, {nome}!");

Console.WriteLine(dobro(21));   // 42
saudar("Ana");                  // Oi, Ana!
```

To the left of `=>` go the parameters; to the right, the result (or a `{ ... }` block). You already used lambdas in LINQ (lesson 14): `Where(x => x > 4)` passes a `Func<int, bool>` to `Where`.

## Closures: lambdas that remember variables

A lambda can use variables from outside it, and it **changes the original variable**:

```csharp
int contador = 0;
Action contar = () => contador++;
contar(); contar();
Console.WriteLine(contador);   // 2
```

## Events: "let me know when it happens"

An **event** is a list of functions to call when something happens. Whoever **publishes** the event doesn't need to know who is listening; whoever **subscribes** just says what to do:

```csharp
class Sensor
{
    public event Action<int, int>? Alerta;    // (index, value)

    public void Registrar(int indice, int valor)
    {
        if (valor > 40) Alerta?.Invoke(indice, valor);
    }
}

var s = new Sensor();
s.Alerta += (i, v) => Console.WriteLine($"Alerta: {v}");   // subscriber 1
s.Alerta += (i, v) => total++;                             // subscriber 2
```

- `+=` subscribes, `-=` unsubscribes;
- `?.Invoke(...)` calls every subscriber, **in the order they subscribed**, and does nothing if nobody subscribed (the event is `null`);
- from outside the class, only `+=` and `-=` are possible: nobody can fire the sensor's alert in its place.

This is the **observer** pattern: interface buttons (`Click`), sensors, games (the enemy announces "I died" and the scoreboard, the sound and the achievements each react on their own) and Unity itself use events all the time. The result is **decoupled** code: you can add a new subscriber without touching the sensor.

## Common mistakes

- Calling `Alerta(…)` without the `?.`: if nobody subscribed, `NullReferenceException`.
- Forgetting that, in `Func`, the return is the **last** type.

cs-17.theory:
# Graphs in C#

A **graph** is a set of **vertices** connected by **edges**: cities and roads, people and friendships, pages and links. In this lesson the vertices have **names** (like `Recife` or `Natal`), and C#'s collections keep the code short.

## Adjacency list

The most common way to store a graph is the **adjacency list**: for each vertex, the list of its neighbours.

```csharp
var grafo = new Dictionary<string, List<string>>();

void Ligar(string a, string b)
{
    if (!grafo.ContainsKey(a)) grafo[a] = new List<string>();
    grafo[a].Add(b);
}

Ligar("Recife", "Natal");
Ligar("Natal", "Recife");   // undirected: connect both ways
```

`Ligar` is a **local function**: declared inside `Main`, it sees its variables. The memory used is proportional to V + E (vertices + edges), much less than a V × V matrix when the graph is sparse, like almost every real graph.

## Breadth-first search (BFS)

**BFS** visits the vertices in waves: first the origin, then its neighbours (distance 1), then the neighbours' neighbours (distance 2)... That is why the first time it reaches a vertex is through the **path with the fewest edges**. The structure that guarantees the waves is the **queue** (`Queue<T>`): the first one in is the first one out.

```csharp
var fila = new Queue<string>();
var pai = new Dictionary<string, string>();
var dist = new Dictionary<string, int> { [origem] = 0 };
fila.Enqueue(origem);
while (fila.Count > 0)
{
    string atual = fila.Dequeue();
    foreach (string viz in grafo[atual])
    {
        if (dist.ContainsKey(viz)) continue;   // already discovered
        dist[viz] = dist[atual] + 1;
        pai[viz] = atual;
        fila.Enqueue(viz);
    }
}
```

The `dist` dictionary plays two roles: it stores the distance and marks who has **already been discovered**. Each vertex enters the queue once and each edge is looked at once (in each direction): cost **O(V + E)**.

## Rebuilding the path

The `pai` dictionary stores, for each vertex, where BFS came from when it discovered it. Starting from the destination and following the parents back to the origin, we get the path **backwards**; we just reverse it:

```csharp
var caminho = new List<string>();
for (string v = destino; v != origem; v = pai[v]) caminho.Add(v);
caminho.Add(origem);
caminho.Reverse();
Console.WriteLine(string.Join(" -> ", caminho));
```

## Which path, if there is a tie?

There may be several paths with the same number of edges. BFS picks one **deterministically**: a vertex is "adopted" by the first one that discovers it, and the order depends on the queue and on the order of the neighbours in the lists (the order in which the edges were read).

## Where this shows up

"People you may know" (friends at distance 2), mathematicians' Erdős number, "Six degrees of separation", routes with the fewest connections between airports and packet searches in networks all use BFS.

cs-19.theory:
# Iterators and yield

Until now, to return several values, a method built a **whole list** and returned it ready. But what if the sequence is huge, or even **infinite**, like "all the prime numbers"? You can't store infinitely many numbers in a list. The solution is to produce the values **on demand**, one at a time, only when someone asks.

## yield return

```csharp
static IEnumerable<int> ContarAte(int n)
{
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
    {
        yield return i;   // hands over i and PAUSES here
    }
}

foreach (int x in ContarAte(3)) Console.WriteLine(x);   // 1, 2, 3
```

A method with `yield return` becomes an **iterator**. It doesn't run when it is called: it returns an object that, every time a `foreach` asks for the next value, runs the method **up to the next `yield return`**, hands over the value and stays **paused** there, with all its local variables preserved. `yield break` ends the sequence.

## Infinite sequences

Since nothing is computed ahead of time, a sequence may have no end:

```csharp
static IEnumerable<long> Primos()
{
    for (long n = 2; ; n++)          // endless loop!
        if (EhPrimo(n)) yield return n;
}
```

This is only safe if the consumer **stops asking**. LINQ has the right operators:

```csharp
Primos().Take(5)                  // 2 3 5 7 11: takes the first 5 and stops
Primos().First(p => p > 100)      // 101: stops at the first one that fits
Primos().TakeWhile(p => p < 20)   // 2 3 5 7 11 13 17 19: stops at the first one that fails
```

Careful: `Primos().Count()` or `Primos().ToList()` never finish, because they try to go through everything.

## Lazy evaluation

This style is called **lazy evaluation**: only what is really used gets computed. LINQ's operators (lesson 14) work like this: `Where`, `Select` and `Take` are iterators that **chain** requests, without creating intermediate lists. In `Primos().Where(p => p % 10 == 3).Take(3)`, `Take` asks `Where`, which asks `Primos`, one value at a time, and everything stops as soon as three values come out.

Advantages: saving memory (a 10 GB file can be read line by line with `File.ReadLines`), results that start showing up right away, and infinite sequences. Disadvantage: going through the same iterator twice **recomputes everything**. If you will reuse it, store it with `.ToList()`.

## The Collatz conjecture

Start with a number `n`. If it is even, divide it by 2; if it is odd, compute `3n + 1`. Repeat until you reach 1. Example: 6, 3, 10, 5, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1. The **Collatz conjecture** says every sequence reaches 1, and nobody has managed to prove it to this day, although it has been checked by computer for gigantic numbers. It is a great example of an iterator: we don't know in advance how many values will come.

```csharp
static IEnumerable<long> Collatz(long n)
{
    while (true)
    {
        yield return n;
        if (n == 1) yield break;
        n = n % 2 == 0 ? n / 2 : 3 * n + 1;
    }
}
```

Use `long`: starting from small numbers, the values climb a lot (from 27, the sequence reaches 9232).

